## 5. Embedding ID Format — Important Inconsistency

| Script | SAR ID format | MS ID format |
|--------|---------------|--------------|
| `generate_baseline_embeddings_adarsh.py` | — | `patch_id` (S2 patch ID, e.g. `S2A_MSIL2A_...`) |
| `generate_s1_val_embeddings_meet.py` | `s1_name` (Sentinel-1 folder name) | — |
| `baseline_retrieval_jainam.py` | reads `baseline_val_s1_patch_ids.txt` | reads `baseline_val_ms_patch_ids.txt` |
| `baseline_ms_to_sar_retrieval_jainam.py` | reads `baseline_val_s1_patch_ids.txt` | reads `baseline_val_patch_ids.txt` |
| `compute_map_week3_adarsh.py` | reads `baseline_val_s1_patch_ids.txt` | reads `baseline_val_ms_patch_ids.txt` |

**Critical path inconsistency** in Jainam's MS→SAR script:
- MS IDs loaded from `baseline_val_patch_ids.txt` (no `ms_` prefix)
- Other scripts use `baseline_val_ms_patch_ids.txt`

The SAR ID format in Meet's output is `s1_name` (Sentinel-1 names), which is different from `patch_id` (S2 patch names). The cross-modal mapping depends on being able to look up the pair from `val_split.csv`. This should work if the retrieval scripts use `val_split.csv` to construct the mapping, which `compute_map_week3_adarsh.py` does correctly.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The `s1_name` IDs in SAR embedding files must be treated as Sentinel-1 folder names, not S2 `patch_id` values. Any script doing a direct string match between SAR IDs and MS IDs in cross-modal retrieval will fail silently — the SAR gallery ID will never match a MS query ID because the naming conventions differ.

